.th PCC I 4/1/77
.sh NAME
pcc \*- interpret Pascal carriage control
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd pcc
[
\fB\*-grnf\fRl
] [ file ... ]
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Pcc
is a filter which interprets Pascal carriage control.
The first character of each  line to
.it Pcc
should be one of the
following carriage control characters:
.s3
.nf
.dt
	` '	single space before printing
	`0'	double space before printing
	`1'	top of form before printing
	`+'	no space before printing
.fi
.s3
.i0
Normally, if the initial character is not a valid control, then a leading
blank is assumed and the line is right shifted with respect to the
rest of the output.
The \fB\*-g\fR (gobble) option causes pcc to, instead, eat non
valid initial characters, which will therefore not show in the output.
.s3
If the \fB\*-f\fR (fold)
option is given then lines which are longer than 132 columns
are folded into multiple lines.
For sensible results when tabs are present, the
fold column should be divisible by 8.
The \fBf\fR may be followed by a count to specify
other widths, i.e.:
.s3
.bd "	pcc \*-f80 output"
.s3
interprets carriage control for the file output with lines longer than 80
columns folded, and
.s3
.bd "	obj | pcc | lpr"
.s3
interprets carriage control for the output of the execution of the pascal object
and puts the result on the line printer.
.s3
The \fB\*-n\fR (no carriage control) option may be used when all that is
desired is folding.
.s3
The \fB\*-r\fR (return) option specifies that the output medium does not
have carriage return capability.
This means that `+' carriage control will
be effected using backspace characters instead of a carriage return character.
.sh "BUGS"
Folded lines followed by `+' carriage control
overprint only the last fold of the line, etc.
